{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554075-splatter-and-gross-out-the-horror-of-w-ta","id":1554075,"title":"Splatter and Gross-out: The Horror of W:tA","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-09-30T23:19:49-07:00","id":1554075,"title":"Splatter and Gross-out: The Horror of W:tA","content":"[Content Warning for realistic simulated bleeding and injury on linked video]<br /> <br /> While there are doubtlessly a lot of videos on the old YT and other streaming sites where people go to town on ballistic gel dummies with arms, I got <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7P1wi0phtAI\" target=\"_blank\">this</a> via algorithm, a recent Sellsword Arts video where they made a point of how much force they <i>weren't</i> using on the by the end very heavily abused and gushing dummy. Given that Garou claws and teeth tend to do about as much damage by the numbers, it's a rather stark illustration of not only what Garou can casually dish out (Crinos would be so much worse than even the planned second video and dummy where they're slated to actually put force behind their sparring strokes), but also what they suffer if they don't have three layers of soak Gifts... and then recover from in a matter of seconds, barring the use of silver, just to suffer it anew.<br /> <br /> (Guns and even explosives tend to be better represented in that kind of video. That might be the reference point for the scale of devastation that Garou inflict in Crinos when they're actually trying.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-01T19:19:19-07:00","id":1554094,"title":"","content":"RPGs in general are very poor representations of how lethal real armed combat is, because real armed combat is <i>extremely</i> lethal. Weapons kill or maim, period, and any minimally competent hit in the actual body by anyone with a weapon is the end for the receiver. Even if the damage isn't life-threatening in the short term, it's a serious wound.<br /> <br /> Hit Points, Hit Boxes, whatever you use, they only make any sense when they somehow represent luck, which is how I like to run it in D&amp;D. The moment you're actually hit, a human should be in a very dire situation.<br /> <br /> Real non-sport combat is mostly defensive, which would be a very boring thing in a game. Most moves are designed to maximize your chances of not being hit instead of focusing on hitting. An &quot;All Out Attack&quot; is actually stupid suicide in almost any circumstance. While women historically didn't participate in the military, it was never because we're weak as weapons actually make it a moot point. In fact strength plays a very minor role in weapon fighting (it plays <i>some</i> role, but it isn't all that important). Historically most people who died as a result of armed conflict did so because of bleeding wounds or sepsis, not because of lethal hits, until the Great War. While traditional armor fell out of use, protection has always been an important thing, but even then a single hit would take someone out of combat more often than not, give or take a few swings before being able to fall back or fall period.<br /> <br /> Even WtA has little sense of how an actual combat runs (keep in mind that all those cuts and thrusts in the video are <b>Lethal</b> for game purposes, not <b>Aggravated</b>), but certainly Garou are combat machines with no regard for how any real living body should react to injury, much less work under injury. And I personally think even the gore art of WtA books underscore how preternaturally grievous are the wounds their claws cause.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-01T23:34:38-07:00","id":1554097,"title":"","content":"Another thing that comes up more in other Sellsword Arts videos (and other HEMA/sword-tube stuff in general, but they're an amusing channel and they do some reality vs. games content too) is that both combatants in a one-on-one fight are very likely to be injured even if it's not an equal trade. Whether blades or guns, if you can attack your opponent, they can attack you, and unless there's a significant disparity in access to meaningful armor, a trained fighter can make sure that even if they lose, their opponent isn't getting away with no injuries that can be (as demonstrated in the link above) quite devastating even if not fatal.<br /> <br /> I wouldn't necessarily say the real world combat is inherently defensive in style, but any real combat training is going to stress avoiding over-commitment to an offensive move (aka the &quot;All Out Attack&quot;) . It's more that being aggressive in real combat is at lot more about seizing initiative (in how it's discussed in HEMA terms and in various older manuals) and taking control of an exchange to maximize your chances of a clean hit without a successful counterattack.<br /> <br /> Another aspect is that exchanges tend to be fairly short before combatants disengage. Again, this holds true with guns as well. Sword fighters don't just keep smashing their weapons against each other (which is a good way to ruin your weapon) and exhaust themselves in prolonged stalemates. What we can glean from history is that most combatants will make 2-4 maneuvers before disengaging. In duels that just means both opponents step out of range and circle a bit to consider another exchange. In military conflicts we know highly coordinated forces would cycle the front line of fighters because a break in the line can doom the whole unit. With ranged weapons you fire a shot or two and duck behind cover instead of staying in one spot and emptying your weapon. <br /> <br /> So I think where this can really play into the horror of the game, is that Garou (though hardly alone in supernatural critters in this respect) can press fights in ways that humans can't. More than the gore, the intensity of an opponent that can take wound after wound and just keep attacking is horrific in a pretty primal way. Yes, gore is nasty, but someone that's actually fought in real combat with swords has seen gore. What they're not prepared for is to see a monster coming at them, with their friends' weapons still lodged in its flesh, moving around with limbs that should be mangled, organs that should be ruptured, and so on.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-02T07:46:15-07:00","id":1554102,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Even WtA has little sense of how an actual combat runs (keep in mind that all those cuts and thrusts in the video are <b>Lethal</b> for game purposes, not <b>Aggravated</b>), but certainly Garou are combat machines with no regard for how any real living body should react to injury, much less work under injury. And I personally think even the gore art of WtA books underscore how preternaturally grievous are the wounds their claws cause.</div> </div> </div> </div>Some forms of agg, at least, probably don't have visual cues to differentiate them from lethal. Garou claws and teeth deal agg, but the only sign of it is if they're fighting another monster that could normally heal.<br /> <br /> As for the massive destruction wreaked by werewolf attacks, especially if they're buffed by effects like Might of Thor, that was why I also mused that what's left is probably more like <a href=\"https://youtu.be/ufEviev5iMM?t=258\" target=\"_blank\">an RPG going off</a> than the aftermath of a typical fight to the death (probably should have posted that link in the OP). And for anyone who's there to see it, it's like that, only instead of being over in a moment like with an explosion, it's going to be drawn out at least a bit more and shower someone with a lot more blood.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong> Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">So I think where this can really play into the horror of the game, is that Garou (though hardly alone in supernatural critters in this respect) can press fights in ways that humans can't. More than the gore, the intensity of an opponent that can take wound after wound and just keep attacking is horrific in a pretty primal way. Yes, gore is nasty, but someone that's actually fought in real combat with swords has seen gore. What they're not prepared for is to see a monster coming at them, with their friends' weapons still lodged in its flesh, moving around with limbs that should be mangled, organs that should be ruptured, and so on.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, also when the aforementioned wave of blood and liquefied flesh hits and there's no explosive evident to explain it in a grounded, readily accepted way (because they can still hear even if they weren't wearing hearing protection, for one).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-02T16:41:08-07:00","id":1554110,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554075-splatter-and-gross-out-the-horror-of-w-ta?p=1554097#post1554097\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What they're not prepared for is to see a monster coming at them, with their friends' weapons still lodged in its flesh, moving around with limbs that should be mangled, organs that should be ruptured, and so on.</div> </div> </div> </div>Even healing (or maybe <i>especially</i> healing) is horrific when it's a <i>thing</i> that should by all means be incapable of fighting, as another important thing in real combat is the notion of stopping power. You're not necessarily trained to kill, but to make your opponent stop fighting back (another thing few RPGs really account for), so an opponent that just <i>won't</i> stop is a veritable nightmare.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554075-splatter-and-gross-out-the-horror-of-w-ta?p=1554102#post1554102\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Some forms of agg, at least, probably don't have visual cues to differentiate them from lethal.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're right. For some reason I tend to think of supernatural claws as causing something like the damage of serrated and/or blunt edges, with horribly irregular instead of clean cuts, something that has somewhat obvious reasons to not heal easy. But that's a big me thing, probably for excess of trying to visualize such damage. It's probable that it's just <b>Lethal</b>-looking most of the time, but hard to heal for unnatural reasons.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554075-splatter-and-gross-out-the-horror-of-w-ta?p=1554102#post1554102\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">that was why I also mused that what's left is probably more like <a href=\"https://youtu.be/ufEviev5iMM?t=258\" target=\"_blank\">an RPG going off</a> than the aftermath of a typical fight to the death\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd say it's probably something in between. An explosion like that basically sprays your liquefied body all over. A Garou attack turns you into an abstract painting on nearby surfaces, as the impossible strength still comes in a specific vector. The result isn't just horrific, it's all the more bizarre the more someone with actual training examines the scene.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, this is also all the more reason why werewolves should be careful about the evidence they leave behind. Delirium takes care of most witnesses of the actual events, but it is the small evidence that can actually cause the wrong type of person to figure out that something <i>very weird</i> is going on.\u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-03T15:51:27-07:00","id":1554132,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554075-splatter-and-gross-out-the-horror-of-w-ta?p=1554110#post1554110\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You're right. For some reason I tend to think of supernatural claws as causing something like the damage of serrated and/or blunt edges, with horribly irregular instead of clean cuts, something that has somewhat obvious reasons to not heal easy. But that's a big me thing, probably for excess of trying to visualize such damage. It's probable that it's just <b>Lethal</b>-looking most of the time, but hard to heal for unnatural reasons.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, yes, but the jagged edge is just a part of being biological. Claws and other dangerous body parts leave jagged wounds because they aren't blades with uniformly sharp edges. This will also potentially give away that it wasn't a knife that did it, since it'll leave a wound or several parallel-ish lines of wounds that look like stylized morse code notation, possibly unless the damage is high enough or you roll enough successes to hit.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'd say it's probably something in between. An explosion like that basically sprays your liquefied body all over. A Garou attack turns you into an abstract painting on nearby surfaces, as the impossible strength still comes in a specific vector. The result isn't just horrific, it's all the more bizarre the more someone with actual training examines the scene.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, this is also all the more reason why werewolves should be careful about the evidence they leave behind. Delirium takes care of most witnesses of the actual events, but it is the small evidence that can actually cause the wrong type of person to figure out that something <i>very weird</i> is going on. </div> </div> </div>I'd say it depends on which effects you're using. If you reach the point where you start doing more dice of damage than a Mark 81 bomb via the contribution from Strength alone... it might start resulting in collateral damage like a Mark 81 bomb. Gurahl probably don't feed Rage into Strength to the max very often for exactly that reason.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-07T10:57:20-07:00","id":1554227,"title":"","content":"TRIGGER WARNING FOR VIOLENCE <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> bkboxer, pugilist , ex bouncher and high content wolfdog owner here. <br /> <br /> Body damage by garou should be something that leaves you with PTSD. I saw many nasty shit, from serious knife wounds, broken noses, front jaw splitted inside the mouth, face exploding blood-sacks, serious eyes injuries, a hundred of different knockouts, various blade injuries. <br /> My high content wolfdog can easily shred my skin if he plays heavy with his claws (and for a wolf claws are not a weapon), and when he bites for playtime he always leaves hematoma or holes. Ww developers doesnt even know how much damage can deliver an adult american gray wolf. They have an enormous strength. At 4 months he was able to dominate a full grown maremman shepherd. <br /> <br /> The damage caused by claws of garou is stuff of nightmare. Probably, the damage of a full blow, should be able to break bones only with the impact. The claws of a wolf are not designed as weapons, but in crinos, I imagine the standard wolf claws but bigger and much larger at the edge then standard talons. Wich means an attack should not even leave claw marks on a body, but literally destroy it, from internal damage and high pressure. The crinos claws in my opinion would probably be able to ravage flesh and bones, not to scratch or cut. <br /> A bite ? For how crinos or hispo are described and thinking about my experience with high content wolves (i work with them ) , you shouldnt even roll to kill a human. Dead and gone. <br /> A single crinos bite should be able to rip off and maim almost everything made of flesh and bones. Wolf teeth are designed in order to do 2 things: <br /> - with their neck resistance and power, snapping necks.<br /> - frontal teeth are all saw-like. They are made to rend flesh and tear it to pieces. Lateral teeth are more deadly then canines and frontal. Those are the teeth that deliver death and lethal blood loss.<br /> <br /> <br /> In my chronicles I always spend time on the gore aspect of the game during explosion of werewolf violence. <br /> people really don't realize often the horror and shock behind this aspect of the game. <br /> <br /> Ive got many bites and injuries. Wta books never satisfied me about the depiction of violent. Too cartoonish. I understand it for sensibility purposes of course. <br /> <br /> But the truth : stuff that gives you ptsd.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#800000\">-'' </span>We are the unsullied.<br /> We are the inheritors.<br /> We are the Pure <span style=\"color:#800000\">''-</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3773-helur","name":"Helur","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3773_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"352"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-11T06:32:38-07:00","id":1554301,"title":"","content":"I already see the damage a manned wolf can due to a person (a zookeeper used to say it was just a &quot;big dog&quot;, until the day the animal attack him). I can imagine what a gray wolf bite looks like. Totally agree with you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10047-vis\u00e3o-de-\u00e1guia","name":"Vis\u00e3o-de-\u00c1guia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10047_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-11T10:39:55-07:00","id":1554303,"title":"","content":"Well, as <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar5826_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"5826\" data-vbnamecard=\"5826\">monteparnas</a> noted in the second post in the thread, the ST system tends to downplay all damage. It's just that here, in the splatter game full of shapeshifters who can turn into dangerous wild predators, it's most noticeable. The themes of other games might not do so well if they had to deal with, say, battle scar results for every injury past a certain threshold because vampires and mages are comparatively fragile.<br /> <br /> Though, really, nowadays, I would kind of like to see something like that to counter how W5 was flattened to make it perform within Vampire's thematic parameters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-11T14:09:46-07:00","id":1554305,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10047-vis\u00e3o-de-\u00e1guia\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar10047_1.jpg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"10047\" data-vbnamecard=\"10047\">Vis\u00e3o-de-\u00c1guia</a> It sounds a bit like someone was underestimating what large breed dogs can do to a human more than not. &quot;Just a big dog&quot; isn't the worst description for a maned wolf's biting power (keeping in mind the maned wolves are not in the same branch of the canids as &quot;true&quot; wolves and most of their relatives are called foxes even though the group isn't in the &quot;true&quot; fox branch either)... but a big dog can easily kill a human if they really mean it.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar23_3.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"23\" data-vbnamecard=\"23\">Saur Ops Specialist</a> An interested way I think you could balance some more impactful damage dealing without turning the game into a meatgrinder would be to do something like a Fated Injury from Scion 2e. While Fated Injuries directly would be a bit not-dark for a WoD game, it could turn Battle Scars into something a bit more narrative/player-directed instead of just randomness from the dice. Basically, I'm imagining something like a Garou player being able to opt to mitigate (some) damage (even potentially fatal damage for a Garou) in exchange for an appropriately scaled Battle Scar. It gives the players some security against PC death in a more damage-impactful game, but doesn't negate consequences of being reckless and eventually PCs that don't get their acts together regarding tactics will end up a mangled mess of scars that can't do much and risks the Rite of the Winter Wolf far younger than intended.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-11T15:13:47-07:00","id":1554306,"title":"","content":"I hate that I'll have to read Scion 2e now to check that out. As an actual contribution to the thread, I could also endorse use of the collateral damage rules from Aberrant 2e. They probably won't rack up disasters quite as quickly, though... unless you're playing a Gurahl or Mokole with the size-increasing Archid traits.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-12T02:54:20-07:00","id":1554312,"title":"","content":"You don't really need to break out Scion 2e if you don't want to?<br /> <br /> In Scion, a Fated Injury basically just delays an injury and weaves it into your character's legend. So it looks like your Scion of Tyr was about to get his hand chopped off, but miraculously the blow fails in the last moment, but now you're destined to lose your hand some time latter as is narratively impactful. Mechanically you resolve an attack as usual, but instead of taking any damage from it, it becomes a thing your ST can inflict on you automatically at another time. All of the Scion specific stuff is just orienting it towards the whole apotheosis arch of that game in how the consequences play out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-17T00:40:29-07:00","id":1554400,"title":"","content":"Had problems with my computer and couldn't come earlier.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554075-splatter-and-gross-out-the-horror-of-w-ta?p=1554132#post1554132\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'd say it depends on which effects you're using. If you reach the point where you start doing more dice of damage than a Mark 81 bomb via the contribution from Strength alone... it might start resulting in collateral damage like a Mark 81 bomb. Gurahl probably don't feed Rage into Strength to the max very often for exactly that reason.</div> </div> </div> </div>The difference (and it's only important as far as it plays on <i><b>my</b></i> preferred take) is the force vector. You can stipulate that the claw has the same momentum of an explosion (which isn't necessarily true as damage is a more abstract thing), but the vector still gives away that something very distinct from an explosion happened.<br /> <br /> But again, this kind of &quot;realism&quot; is merely a matter of style. It's because I like the idea that sometimes someone catches on such details and this becomes a problem to be dealt with. I like detective stories, so detective tropes are always welcome on my games.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Helur</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554075-splatter-and-gross-out-the-horror-of-w-ta?p=1554227#post1554227\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">TRIGGER WARNING FOR VIOLENCE<br /> <br /> Body damage by garou should be something that leaves you with PTSD.</div> </div> </div> </div>Regular weapon damage should be something that gives you PTSD. Again, RPGs in general and WoD in particular terribly downplay how fragile the human body really is (and also is not, but that's for another time).<br /> <br /> But that said, I'll play a bit of devil's advocate and say you have to keep in mind the abstraction of the game.<br /> <br /> At the end of the day, damage isn't about what exactly happened to your body, but how much it affects your ability to function and/or remain alive. Likewise, hitting isn't about actually connecting an attack, but managing to impair the target's ability to function. Of course this all still means you connect attacks and cause injuries, but measuring those isn't the actual goal (or realistically feasible).<br /> <br /> What that means is, as an example, that a hit by a Garou's bite that didn't cause enough damage to kill a human doesn't have to mean the Garou bit but didn't kill, it may be the human mostly avoided it, yet either brushing with the Garou or the sheer shock of the experience was enough to affect them.\u200b<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vis\u00e3o-de-\u00c1guia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554075-splatter-and-gross-out-the-horror-of-w-ta?p=1554301#post1554301\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I already see the damage a manned wolf can due to a person (a zookeeper used to say it was just a &quot;big dog&quot;, until the day the animal attack him). I can imagine what a gray wolf bite looks like. Totally agree with you.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm with <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> here, it seems a bit off. Lobo Guar\u00e1s aren't known for a strong bite <i><b>for a canid</b></i>, quite the opposite, it's weaker than a dog of the same size. But a dog can still cause <b>a lot</b> of harm, especially large breeds. Most dog bites we usually see aren't neither by large breeds nor full force.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-19T11:21:34-07:00","id":1554481,"title":"","content":"Yeah... I'm totally fail in understanding what you guys are &quot;criticizing&quot; in what I writted, but ok...<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":10047,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10047-vis\u00e3o-de-\u00e1guia","user":"Vis\u00e3o-de-\u00c1guia","date":". 10-19-2025, 02:26 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10047-vis\u00e3o-de-\u00e1guia","name":"Vis\u00e3o-de-\u00c1guia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10047_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-19T16:14:50-07:00","id":1554497,"title":"","content":"At this point I think we've arrived at the dilemma of how to present damage when the narrative bent of W:tA is to chunkify characters casually and turn them into meat on the ground.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-22T20:16:22-08:00","id":1555783,"title":"","content":"The end of the Hunter: the Parenting episode &quot;Feuds of our Fathers&quot; comes to mind, where they find the remains of poor Fatigue.<br /> <br /> And yeah, I agree with Helur on this one: if you roll for your claw or bite attack to strike a mortal dude and it does with more than 2 success, then that human is GG unless they have either a dodge or soak pool larger than four dice - plus, perhaps either wearing quality armor or have Relics that can boost either of those dice pools.<br /> <br /> <br /> Plus, there's a whole aspect of post-fight RP to go with. &quot;So, you DID survive getting your rib cage caved in by a 9-foot tall furry chainsaw with legs and anger issues! Now what?&quot;<br /> There's a whole bit where recovering from Lethal and Aggravated injuries can cripple the pacing, but this can be alleviated by giving it a humorous mood (provided you're careful and don't over-rely on comedy) or speeding up the process by having an npc that specializes in the field of &quot;TOTALLY SCIENTIFIC METHODS OF ADVANCED HEALING - IT'S NOT MAGIC OR SORCERY! SHUT UP&quot; come in and speed up the healing process. lol<br /> <br /> But yeah - most Garou usually can (and do) obliterate a single, average mortal. And the nightmares that Garou fight against are also strong enough to one-shot them in-turn. In my experience with WoD's combat, everything is either a glass cannon or a tank that can't land his shots and won't die, and with zero in-between.<br /> <br /> I remember one time, I had a player roll to attack a Black Spiral Dancer, as both were in Crinos. The player got 15 total successes, due to constantly rolling 10's on his dice. He wanted to give up after rolling 9 successes, but I forced him to keep adding dice and rolling on top of his 10's, because I wanted to see how ridiculous this would get.<br /> <br /> Laughing, and wanting this to be a highlight for the player, I simply said &quot;You pick up the BSD and rip him in half.&quot; Some of the luck at our table was bonkers. lol","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28838,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28838-dafrontlinespecter","user":"DaFrontlineSpecter","date":".  grammar mistakes. Got it. 11-22-2025, 11:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28838-dafrontlinespecter","name":"DaFrontlineSpecter","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"24"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-22T21:59:06-08:00","id":1555787,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DaFrontlineSpecter</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1554075-splatter-and-gross-out-the-horror-of-w-ta?p=1555783#post1555783\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The end of the Hunter: the Parenting episode &quot;Feuds of our Fathers&quot; comes to mind, where they find the remains of poor Fatigue.<br /> <br /> And yeah, I agree with Helur on this one: if you roll for your claw or bite attack to strike a mortal dude and it does with more than 2 success, then that human is GG unless they have either a dodge or soak pool larger than four dice - plus, perhaps either wearing quality armor or have Relics that can boost either of those dice pools.<br /> <br /> <br /> Plus, there's a whole aspect of post-fight RP to go with. &quot;So, you DID survive getting your rib cage caved in by a 9-foot tall furry chainsaw with legs and anger issues! Now what?&quot;<br /> There's a whole bit where recovering from Lethal and Aggravated injuries can cripple the pacing, but this can be alleviated by giving it a humorous mood (provided you're careful and don't over-rely on comedy) or speeding up the process by having an npc that specializes in the field of &quot;TOTALLY SCIENTIFIC METHODS OF ADVANCED HEALING - IT'S NOT MAGIC OR SORCERY! SHUT UP&quot; come in and speed up the healing process. lol</div> </div> </div> </div><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=17-7x3Efnic\" target=\"_blank\">A genie did it!</a><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But yeah - most Garou usually can (and do) obliterate a single, average mortal. And the nightmares that Garou fight against are also strong enough to one-shot them in-turn. In my experience with WoD's combat, everything is either a glass cannon or a tank that can't land his shots and won't die, and with zero in-between.<br /> <br /> I remember one time, I had a player roll to attack a Black Spiral Dancer, as both were in Crinos. The player got 15 total successes, due to constantly rolling 10's on his dice. He wanted to give up after rolling 9 successes, but I forced him to keep adding dice and rolling on top of his 10's, because I wanted to see how ridiculous this would get.<br /> <br /> Laughing, and wanting this to be a highlight for the player, I simply said &quot;You pick up the BSD and rip him in half.&quot; Some of the luck at our table was bonkers. lol </div> </div> </div>Reminds me of the Rage CCG combat card &quot;Cleft-in-Twain&quot;, the most powerful damaging card in the game. The illustration is <a href=\"https://www.ebay.com/itm/176634121336\" target=\"_blank\">exactly what it says</a>.<br /> <br /> (The Zmei I reference for different reasons. Always remember to be clear with your art direction and never just assume what people mean when you tell them it's &quot;a serpent-dragon biting some clown in half.&quot;)<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}